<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Knidos</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.) · capital Knidos</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Dorian port city on a Carian peninsula, home of Praxiteles's Aphrodite, the astronomer Eudoxus and a renowned school of medicine.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorians">Dorian Greeks</a> settled Knidos at the tip of a long peninsula in south-west <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anatolia">Anatolia</a> in the early first millennium BCE. It belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dorian-hexapolis">Dorian Hexapolis</a> with Kos, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/halicarnassus">Halicarnassus</a> and the three Rhodian cities of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lindos">Lindos</a>, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/ialysos">Ialysos</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kamiros">Kamiros</a>, which met at the sanctuary of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> at Triopion in Knidian territory.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Knidos was an oligarchy run by a council of sixty, and grew rich on trade, wine and its double harbour. When the Persian general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harpagus">Harpagus</a> advanced c. 545 BCE, the Knidians tried to cut through their isthmus but submitted. The city later produced the astronomer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eudoxus-of-cnidus">Eudoxus</a>, the physician <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ctesias">Ctesias</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sostratus-of-cnidus">Sostratus</a>, architect of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lighthouse-of-alexandria">Pharos of Alexandria</a>; its treasury and clubhouse stood at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/delphi">Delphi</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Off Knidos in 394 BCE <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conon">Conon's</a> Persian-backed fleet destroyed Spartan sea power. In the 4th century BCE the city moved to the cape, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/praxiteles">Praxiteles's</a> nude Aphrodite drew visitors. It joined the Roman province of Asia in 129 BCE as a free city.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/knidos.jpg" alt="Knidos" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Tischbeinahe · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Knidos-H%C3%A4fen.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 129 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Knidos</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Aphrodite</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Doric Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Turkey</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cnidus</dd></dl></aside></div>

